[
    {
        "content": "<p>We have currently a diamond around rigid categories. I mean, a real diamond where the instances are not defeq.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Category</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">MonoidalCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">BraidedCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">RightRigidCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">BraidedCategory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">rigidCategoryOfRightRigidCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">RigidCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">BraidedCategory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">rigidCategoryOfLeftRigidCategory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">--fails</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Is that a problem, or can we live with it? This comes from the fact that a right-rigid category gives a rigid category, and a right-rigid category gives a left-rigid category, and a left-rigid category gives a rigid category, but the triangle doesn't match definitionally. </p>\n<p>Given one can go freely from right-rigid to left-rigid and conversely, is that really relevant to have three classes <code>RightRigid</code>, <code>LeftRigid</code>, and <code>Rigid</code>? Should we just have <code>Rigid</code>, say, and various constructors building it either from data amounting to left rigidity, or from data amounting to right rigidity?</p>\n<p>Disclaimer: I don't know anything about category theory and rigid categories, these are just random words for me.</p>",
        "id": 584644415,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775817901
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Or should we just kill one side of the triangle, for example the instance <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=CategoryTheory.BraidedCategory.rigidCategoryOfRightRigidCategory%3F#doc\">docs#CategoryTheory.BraidedCategory.rigidCategoryOfRightRigidCategory?</a> Since one can follow the two other sides, we would still get all the instances. But this would break arbitrarily the symmetry between left-rigid and right-rigid.</p>",
        "id": 584645479,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775818260
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, it’s a problem. All of these kind of declarations  in <code>Mathlib/CategoryTheory/Monoidal/Rigid/Braided.lean</code> should not be instances.</p>",
        "id": 584645780,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Carlier",
        "timestamp": 1775818369
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would think we should remove the instances and have them as <code>abbrev</code> instead. (These notions are distinct in general, but \"coincide\" only for braided categories.)</p>",
        "id": 584645835,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1775818388
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Would something like <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37881\">#37881</a> be ok?</p>",
        "id": 584647183,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775818891
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Another non-defeq diamond in category theory:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">instCommRingCarrierTensorUnit</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">Ring</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">MonoidalCategoryStruct</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">tensorUnit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">MonModuleEquivalenceAlgebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">MonObj</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toRing</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">MonoidalCategoryStruct</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">tensorUnit</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span><span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fails</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Any idea on this one?</p>",
        "id": 584648584,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775819405
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would think <code>ModuleCat.MonModuleEquivalenceAlgebra.MonObj.toRing</code> should only be a scoped instance.</p>",
        "id": 584649089,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1775819590
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The fact that they're not defeq (really not defeq, it's not a problem of transparency) probably means that one of these instances has something wrong in it, no?</p>",
        "id": 584649340,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775819691
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Or if the ring structure is expected to be genuinely different, then shouldn't it be on a type synonym?</p>",
        "id": 584649448,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775819738
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry, the ring structures are indeed the same. Just not definitionally.</p>",
        "id": 584650992,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775820325
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The issue is with the <code>npow</code> field. When going from the ring to the monoidal object, one has to forget it because there is no analogous field in the definition of <code>MonObj</code>. </p>\n<p>To fix the diamond, one could add such a field in the definition of <code>MonObj</code>, completely parallel to <code>npow</code> (with prop fields registering that it behaves as it should, and a default data field).</p>\n<p>Or we can live with the diamond, scoping the instance as you suggest, and adding a warning somewhere.</p>",
        "id": 584653372,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775821197
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Since <code>Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Monoidal.Internal.Module</code> (where this instance is defined) is a leaf file (and has been so for years it seems), scoping this instance and living with it is probably the easiest solution for the time being.</p>\n<p>The idea of having an <code>npow</code> field for monoid objects is nice. Though I am not sure if there are many other situations where this would be useful (but perhaps there are? e.g. the same would arise if we want to compare monoid internal to chain complexes and things like dg-algebras?).</p>",
        "id": 584659001,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Carlier",
        "timestamp": 1775823100
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37883\">#37883</a> scopes the instances</p>",
        "id": 584662433,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775824154
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would think we do not need to add <code>npow</code> fields for monoid objects. For example, for an abelian variety <code>G</code>, it is important to be able to consider the \"multiplication by <code>n</code>\" as a (group) scheme morphism from <code>G</code> to <code>G</code>, but this <code>n/zsmul</code> (or <code>n/zpow</code>) can perfectly be a <code>def</code> rather than a field of the typeclasses <code>MonObj</code> or <code>GrpObj</code>.</p>",
        "id": 584664020,
        "sender_full_name": "Joël Riou",
        "timestamp": 1775824649
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The only interest to have it a field is to fix diamonds like the above. Otherwise, a def is perfectly fine, yes.</p>",
        "id": 584664320,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1775824728
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>While reviewing <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37814\">#37814</a> by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"348873\">@Saul Glasman</span> , it seems that this ring structure is causing another trouble: when <code>M</code> is a <code>MonObj</code>, there are now non-defeq multiplicative structures on <code>M ⊗ M</code>: the one from being the tensor product of two monoid objects, and the one from being the tensor product of two rings:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">CategoryTheory</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MonoidalCategory</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">/--</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">error: Type mismatch</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  rfl</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">has type</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  ?m.93 = ?m.93</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">but is expected to have type</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">  a ⊗ₜ[R] b * c ⊗ₜ[R] d = a ⊗ₜ[R] b * c ⊗ₜ[R] d</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">-/</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">guard_msgs</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ModuleCat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">MonObj</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">HMul</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">hMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">TensorProduct</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">TensorProduct</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">TensorProduct</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">instHMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">TensorProduct</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">TensorProduct</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">instMul</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗ₜ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗ₜ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">HMul</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">hMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"n\">instHMul</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">↑</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">M</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ring</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toNonAssocRing</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toNonUnitalNonAssocSemiring</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toDistrib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">toMul</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗ₜ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗ₜ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It seems the API for the monoidal structure on <code>ModuleCat R</code> abuses a fair amount the defeq <code>M ⊗ N = M ⊗[R] N</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585190410,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Carlier",
        "timestamp": 1776086993
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>are you talking about <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ModuleCat.MonoidalCategory.tensorObj_carrier#doc\">docs#ModuleCat.MonoidalCategory.tensorObj_carrier</a> ?</p>",
        "id": 585217723,
        "sender_full_name": "Edison Xie",
        "timestamp": 1776093584
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, <code>tensorObj_carrier</code> is one of the defeq that gets a bit abused here (in the sense that at semi-reducible transparency, lemmas like <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=ModuleCat.MonoidalCategory.whiskerLeft_apply#doc\">docs#ModuleCat.MonoidalCategory.whiskerLeft_apply</a> are \"abusing\" the defeq AFAIU). Above, the issue is the combination of this defeq with the <code>Ring</code> structure one gets when <code>M</code> is a <code>MonObj</code>.</p>",
        "id": 585220601,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Carlier",
        "timestamp": 1776094350
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>does tagging the monoidal category instance with <code>@[reducible]</code> help?</p>",
        "id": 585221260,
        "sender_full_name": "Edison Xie",
        "timestamp": 1776094531
    }
]